{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790286279,"nonce":"PnslCthv+NzVF0PCksAcK+0NdpE=","title":"Deep non-tail recursion whose first call lands on a quick baseline overflows the stack","message":"A stub's first call of a loop-free program function publishes a quick baseline (the interpreter's body at tier 0) to the stub, never to the call cell, so each recursive call through the cell goes cell -\u003e stub -\u003e __zyntax_lazy_compile -\u003e quick code until the worker's replacement lands. The quick body also lacks tco's marks. Frames of a recursion entered during that window stay in the quick code (and carry a stub frame per level) until the whole recursion returns.\n\nFound verifying package C-baseline-first-loop-free: zypy aborts with a stack overflow at a non-tail recursion depth of 300000 or more when the recursion's first call lands on a quick baseline, where the 8844ca79 binary succeeds up to 1000000. Not conformance-visible (CPython stops at 1000 by default; Lua matches lua5.4 at its limits).\n\nOptions: keep self-recursive functions (or members of a call-graph SCC) off the quick path, or have the quick path not add a stub frame per level.","files":null}]}